From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 07:59:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] coremessaging: timers, dedicated threads, and shutting a
 queue down.

A dispatcher queue could run what was queued on it and nothing more:
CreateTimer failed, a dedicated thread was the calling thread,
ShutdownQueueAsync completed without doing anything, and the two
shutdown events were never raised.  Windows.UI.Composition draws its
animations' frames with a queue's timer, and WinAppSDK code shuts its
queues down, so all of it is now there.

A timer ticks through a window timer on the queue's window, on the
queue's thread, whichever thread starts or stops it.  As measured on
Windows: a new timer has no interval, repeats and is not running;
IsRunning is FALSE while its handlers run, even for a repeating timer;
a negative interval is E_INVALIDARG and leaves the interval as it was;
a handler may stop its own timer; and the queue holds no reference to a
timer, so one released while running stops.

A dedicated thread makes its queue, runs its messages, and holds a
reference to the queue until the queue shuts down.  A shutdown is a
message posted to the queue, so what was queued before runs first;
ShutdownStarting's handlers can hold it with a deferral; then the queue
takes nothing more, TryEnqueue saying so with FALSE rather than
failing, the operation completes, ShutdownCompleted is raised, and a
dedicated thread ends.  The operation is completed before that event,
as the tests expect of it.

The tests of the existing file now pass without their todos, and new
ones for the timer pass on Windows as they do here.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 4566ae2e1288e24f6a58a14cb5daebc9d764d779)

